<h2>The marriage and its breakdown</h2>

<p>The parties married on 20 October 2010 and registered the marriage before the Marriage Officer at Jamshedpur under the Special Marriage Act, 1954, while also performing Hindu rites. Two children were born, in 2012 and 2015. On the wife's case, the marriage deteriorated after the second child: the husband drank heavily, abused and assaulted her, and after his father's death in 2015 stopped working and supporting the family altogether. On 15 March 2018 she was driven out of the matrimonial home with the children. She has since lived with her parents, supporting the household on her earnings as a nurse, and lodged a police complaint in February 2024 before filing for divorce.</p>

<p>Despite valid service, the husband never appeared — not before the Additional Principal Judge, Additional Family Court No. 1, Jamshedpur, and not before the High Court. The Family Court nonetheless dismissed her suit (Original Suit No. 59 of 2024) by judgment of 9 February 2026, unimpressed by the absence of contemporaneous police action and treating her long stay at her parental home as desertion on her part.</p>

<h2>Where the Family Court went wrong</h2>

<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><h3>Family Court</h3><p>No FIR at the time of the alleged assaults, so the cruelty evidence is doubtful; the wife left in 2018 and stayed away, so she is the deserting spouse.</p></div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new"><h3>High Court</h3><p>Non-institution of an FIR cannot by itself discredit sworn testimony — the finding was perverse. Departure compelled by cruelty is not wilful abandonment; the desertion operates against the spouse who caused it.</p></div>
</div>

<p>On cruelty, the Bench — Justice Sujit Narayan Prasad writing — held that the Family Court's appreciation of evidence was perverse: the mere fact of non-institution of a formal First Information Report cannot, by itself, be treated as a ground to discredit the wife's testimony. Indian matrimonial reality supplies the reason. Spouses, particularly those with children, routinely endure abuse for years without criminalising the household; the law of divorce does not demand that they should have.</p>

<p>On desertion, the Court applied the classical two-element test — the factum of separation and the animus deserendi, persisting through the statutory period — and found both pointing at the husband. The wife's departure from the matrimonial home could not be construed as a wilful abandonment of the husband; it was, in the Court's words, under compelling circumstances and not voluntary. A spouse compelled to leave by cruelty is the victim of constructive desertion, not its author.</p>

<h2>The ex parte dimension</h2>

<p>The husband's complete absence gave the appeal an unusual texture: there was no rival narrative at all, only the Family Court's own scepticism standing between the wife and a decree. The judgment is a reminder that even in uncontested matrimonial cases the petitioner must prove the ground — matrimonial relief does not follow by default — but equally that judicial scrutiny of an unrebutted, consistent account must stay within the bounds of reasonable appreciation. Scepticism untethered from the record is perversity, and a Division Bench hearing a first appeal under Section 19(1) of the Family Courts Act, 1984 will interfere.</p>

<div class="check">
  <p>Appeal allowed; the judgment of 9 February 2026 and decree of 16 February 2026 quashed and set aside.</p>
  <p>The marriage dissolved under Section 27(1)(b) and (d) of the Special Marriage Act, 1954; the Registry directed to draw up the decree of dissolution.</p>
  <p>The children's inheritance rights expressly preserved.</p>
  <p>Liberty reserved to the wife to claim maintenance for the minor children in the appropriate forum.</p>
</div>

<h2>What practitioners should take from it</h2>

<p>Three working rules emerge. First, plead and prove the compulsion behind any departure from the matrimonial home — dates, incidents, witnesses, and the financial reality that followed — because the desertion analysis turns on who caused the separation, not who physically walked out. Second, do not let the absence of an FIR dominate the narrative; contemporaneous complaints help, but their absence is explicable and the High Court has now said so in terms. Third, in ex parte matrimonial trials, build the record as though the case were contested: it is the court, not the absent respondent, that must be satisfied, and a thin record invites exactly the dismissal that occurred here before it was corrected in appeal.</p>

<div class="note"><p>Delhi practice note: appeals from Family Court decrees in Delhi lie to a Division Bench of the Delhi High Court under Section 19 of the Family Courts Act, 1984, ordinarily within thirty days. The grounds of cruelty and desertion under Section 13 of the Hindu Marriage Act are construed on materially identical principles.</p></div>
